    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Metformin is usually the traditional first-line treatment for type 2 diabetes, but health guidelines now recommend choosing either an SGLT2 inhibitor or a GLP-1 receptor agonist early on if you have specific heart, kidney, or weight-related conditions.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "SGLT2 inhibitor",
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/diabetes-type-2/glp-1-vs-sglt2"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "health guidelines now recommend choosing either an SGLT2 inhibitor or a GLP-1 receptor agonist early on if you have specific heart, kidney, or weight-related conditions"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The right choice depends on your personal health needs and medical history."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Blood Sugar and Weight Loss"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists: Lower your HbA1c (average blood sugar) by up to 1.5% to 2% and promote major weight loss (about 9 to 13 pounds)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors: Lower HbA1c by up to 1% and cause moderate weight loss (about 2 to 6 pounds)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Heart and Kidney Protection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors: Excel at protecting the kidneys and reducing hospitalizations or death from heart failure. They are the top choice if you have existing heart failure."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists: Excel at preventing major heart events, heart attacks, and strokes, making them ideal if you have high cardiovascular risk."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How You Take Them and Side Effects"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Form: SGLT2 inhibitors are taken as a daily oral pill. Most GLP-1 receptor agonists are given as weekly or daily injections, though oral forms are available."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side effects: GLP-1 drugs often cause stomach issues like nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea. SGLT2 drugs can increase the risk of genital and urinary tract infections."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Further Exploration"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Read a full comparison on GoodRx."
